## 2. Cross-cutting constraints all candidates inherit

These are not options. They are consequences of the corpus, and two of them contradict things currently written down. Each candidate is scored against them in §3.

**CC-1 — L07 is unconditional in the kernel, and the state machine says otherwise.**
`operations/WORKFLOW_STATE_MACHINE.yaml:179` gives L06 two exits: `{L07: "matrix has material risks", L08: "no material risk"}`. The kernel lattice does not: the intake records `is_legal(TRADEOFF, DESIGN, SUBSTANTIAL) == False`. The YAML's "no material risk" bypass is **unimplementable without a kernel edit**, which is forbidden. Therefore *every* candidate must execute L07 on every substantial run, including runs with an empty risk set, and must emit a catalogue artifact in the empty case (an attested "no high-severity trade-offs" record). A candidate that keeps a conditional-skip path is non-conforming. This also means the acceptance test's "NO direct TRADEOFF→DESIGN edge" is not merely a preference — it is the only legal walk.

**CC-2 — Trace-A auto-discharge still needs a real artifact to bind to.**
`PAEOS-9:745` and `ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md:29` require an `{auto_pass, trace_a, runtime}` stub *so every transition stays four-tuple-valid*. But SI-4 binding (`PAEOS-7.6 §6`) rejects evidence whose `artifact_hash` ≠ the artifact under review. A stub with no artifact has nothing to bind to. So even Trace-A must write a minimal catalogue object to the CAS and bind the stub evidence to its hash. This keeps the acceptance clause "emits a catalogue artifact with a bound `artifact_hash`" true on *every* run, not just Trace-B runs — a strictly better acceptance test than one that only holds on the deep path.

**CC-3 — Coverage is not adequacy; that is where CANARY-0002 reappears.**
BC-3 forbids an always-passing stage. The obvious fix (assert every high-severity row has a non-empty `safeguard` field) converts an always-pass into a *trivially satisfiable* pass: `safeguard: "TBD"` clears it. The detection signature of CANARY-0002 is "identical result whether the artifact is applied or not"; the trivially-satisfiable variant is "identical result whether the safeguard is real or a placeholder". Any candidate must state which of the two it defeats and admit the other as debt.

**CC-4 — Judgment evidence needs two producers; deterministic evidence needs a re-runnable command.**
`PAEOS-7.6 §6` gate rules: deterministic evidence is kernel-re-run; **non-deterministic evidence requires two independent producers within tolerance**. A single-session "the planner asserts the safeguards are adequate" is neither: it is unrepeatable *and* single-producer. This constrains how the obligation may be typed (`TEST` vs `CITATION`) and is the strongest structural argument for candidate B.

**CC-5 — The escalation route is deliberately a stop.**
`ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md:111`: irreversible accepted-risks → founder Decision. `PAEOS-9 §8.4`: blocking is safe indefinitely, the clock is paused, `blocked` never alters `status` and never itself triggers BUDGET_EXCEEDED. BC-5 accepts this. Per DC-10 the escalation must terminate in an artifact amendment (a reusable accepted-risk policy), not a one-off answer, or L07 becomes a recurring interrupt generator.

